  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $7.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7}+\\frac{2y}{7}=1 & |\\,\\times 7 \\\\ x-y=1\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=7 \\\\ x-y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $x$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ x & =1+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $x=1+y$ into the first equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =7 \\\\ (1+y)+2y & =7 \\\\ 1+3y & =7 \\\\ 3y & =6 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x,$ we substitute $y=2$ into the equation $x=1+y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1+y \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3,\\,y=2.$",
